#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193676} :activity/id #uuid "27b7cff9-86aa-5b81-a7d3-2697abb3c99e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050461 :topic/id #uuid "4b1a1cce-e35a-540f-9c78-90e0889cdb1f" :topic/math-academy-id 4116} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192387 :question/id #uuid "c0233eba-1101-5c9f-8603-bbfdabb3923f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10095"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193189}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "b29450d6-7dae-513c-855c-9d127f25ad09" :step/math-academy-id 38067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192388 :question/id #uuid "bac24078-f1fb-56ae-ac2b-baa86b3a75ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-10096"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193188}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"} :step/id #uuid "2db38e99-7855-5515-a956-dbad946ec6df" :step/math-academy-id 38068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i$, and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following:\n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize,\n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$,\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1$, and\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0$.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the intercept parameter $\\beta_0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "109cb2d6-433c-5ebc-8c14-b0db564dcee8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6965 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "140fd64b-240a-5128-bceb-5d2258cb5439" :step/math-academy-id 42156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193892 :tutorial/content "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}_{\\textrm{standard} \\ \\,\\:\\: \\textrm{error}}}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by\n$(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)$.\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2$.\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc1aaf89-d357-53d7-8943-926609018212" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6967 :tutorial/title "Summary of the Key Result"} :step/id #uuid "ac2be2f7-e3fa-5b92-b417-ac33fa6ff9d7" :step/math-academy-id 42158 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)nx^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ_{0}^(^) &= y^(―) - β_{1}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - (∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i})/(S_{xx})x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i} - x^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0}}]$.\n\n- For the mean, we have\n$E[β_{0}^(^)]|= \\frac{1}{n}E[∑_(i)y_{i}] - x^(―)E[∑_(i)k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)E[y_{i}] - x^(―)∑_(i)E[k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)∑_(i)k_{i}(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}); = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)[β_{0}∑_(i)k_{i} + β_{1}∑_(i)k_{i}x_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)[β_{0}∑_(i)k_{i}^{0} + β_{1}∑_(i)k_{i}x_{i}^{1}]; = \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i)(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}) - x^(―)β_{1}; = \\frac{β_{0}}{n}∑_(i)1 + \\frac{β_{1}}{n}∑_(i)x_{i} - x^(―)β_{1}; = \\frac{β_{0}}{n} \\cdot n + x^(―)β_{1} - x^(―)β_{1}; = β_{0}$.\n- For the variance, we have\n$Var[β_{0}^(^)]|= \\frac{1}{n^{2}}Var[∑_(i)y_{i}] + x^(―)^{2}Var[∑_(i)k_{i}y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}∑_(i)Var[y_{i}] + x^(―)^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}Var[y_{i}]; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}nσ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}σ^{2}; = \\frac{1}{n^{2}}nσ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}σ^{2}∑_(i)k_{i}^{2}; = \\frac{1}{n}σ^{2} + x^(―)^{2}σ^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{S_{xx}}; = σ^{2}(\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))$.\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e3cbadc-9a0c-5925-af3f-b47ca4a2ff80" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6968 :tutorial/title "Expectation and Variance of Intercept Parameter Estimates"} :step/id #uuid "2e9c0fd9-3132-5c7d-b2e2-2b04a2530995" :step/math-academy-id 42159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193900 :tutorial/content "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our intercept parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$.\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$.\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nFor each\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nlet\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2$. The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i, \\sigma^2\\right)\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_0 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0, \\: \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2$, the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_0$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_0 -\\beta_0}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}]}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1aef59f3-040d-59b5-90f2-5f746d059c4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7007 :tutorial/title "Deriving an Expression for the Confidence Interval"} :step/id #uuid "e07f8b0d-114e-5676-8692-12b109c4375f" :step/math-academy-id 42309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193678}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for Linear Regression Intercept Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192927}]}
